\description{
\strong{Experimental.} \code{multiScale_joint()} combines two or more data sets, each
already fitted with \code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}}, and estimates the scale of effect
of each landscape covariate jointly. Each data set keeps its own response,
model class, and native parameters (for example a Poisson count model and an
\code{unmarked} occupancy model). What is shared is set by \code{scales} and, if you
choose, \code{share}:
\itemize{
\item \code{scales = "common"}: one scale for each landscape covariate, used by every
data set that contains it.
\item \code{scales = "separate"}: one scale per data set. This reproduces the separate
fits and is the reference for testing whether a common scale is supported.
\item \code{scales = "footprint"}: one ecological scale, seen through each data set's
sampling footprint (see Details). The apparent scale in data set \code{d} is
\code{sqrt(sigma^2 + footprint[d]^2)}.
\item \code{scales = "footprint_scaled"}: as \code{"footprint"}, with the footprint SDs
multiplied by an estimated factor \code{kappa}. \code{kappa = 0} is the common
scale and \code{kappa = 1} is the stated footprint.
}

\code{share} names landscape covariates whose regression coefficient is also
shared across data sets, so the data sets estimate one landscape effect but
keep their own intercepts and other coefficients.
